"""Original non-clinical navigation, handover and service-review teaching."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-060","Mental Health Service Coordination and Safe Referral","mental-health-service-coordination-and-safe-referral","Mental Health Service\nCoordination and\nSafe Referral",
 "Prepare a fictional mental-health support pathway with verified service fit, specific information permissions, accepted handover responsibility and a bounded continuity review within professional scope.",[
m("Needs roles and service fit","Distinguish non-clinical navigation from treatment coordination and specify a service-verification task.",[
"Coordination helps connect a person's needs and preferences with suitable roles across services. It is more than giving someone a long list of contacts. Begin with the person's goal, the barrier to using support and the action each service can actually take. The WHO community guidance links mental-health services with social supports and ordinary community life. An introductory worker may help clarify access questions within their role; making a diagnosis, changing medication, deciding clinical triage or directing treatment requires qualified authority. Do not infer that all activities described as coordination have the same competency requirements. [S21, S1]",
"The 2025 scoping review distinguishes varied clinical and non-clinical tasks described in adult mental-health coordination. Its included settings and eligibility rules differ considerably, and exclusion of case-management terminology may have missed similar models. The review maps concepts and reported experiences rather than proving one effective model for every service. Its examples are not a universal eligibility standard or a substitute for local role definitions. For a fictional plan, state which task is practical navigation, which requires qualified clinical judgement and which belongs to an organisational decision-maker. [R32, S21]",
"A service option needs current verification of scope, eligibility, cost, access, communication and capacity through authorised arrangements. A familiar name or an old directory entry does not establish that a person can use the service now. Consider whether transport, language, disability access, digital exclusion or scheduling affects fit. Do not promise acceptance, a waiting time or a particular outcome before appropriate confirmation. The 2025 policy introduction makes workforce and system conditions relevant to quality; shortages should be represented as a service dependency rather than silently treated as the person's responsibility. [S22, S21, S1]",
"In this exercise, use fictional services and identify what would need verification before an actual referral. Keep a directory-quality question separate from a clinical assessment and do not contact real patients, impersonate a professional or send a real health account as a test. Write the information status clearly: a proposed option, verified service information and acceptance for a particular person are different. A useful outline names the authorised role that will check the gap and the next appropriate decision if the option is unsuitable. A completed worksheet does not qualify an independent care coordinator. [S1, R32, S21]"],
"Fictional case: an adult needs an accessible appointment and transport support. A worker provides an old service list and marks care coordinated. The learner identifies the actual goal, verification questions and separate clinical and practical responsibilities.",
"Prepare a service-fit note with the person's goal, a practical barrier, service-status questions, clinical/non-clinical role distinction, verification owner and a conditional next step.",
"A sound note identifies fit and authority rather than equating a directory with access. It retains uncertainty about current capacity and keeps treatment decisions with qualified roles.",["S21","S1","R32","S22"],[
("Coordination begins with a person's goal and access needs.","A fictional worker sends a long contact list without considering an adult's access barrier. What should be clarified?","The goal, practical barrier and suitable role for the next action.",["Only the length of the directory.","A claim that every listed service is appropriate.","The worker's preferred diagnosis from the list."],"Useful coordination connects needs and feasible actions rather than merely supplying contacts."),
("Navigation and treatment coordination have different authority requirements.","A support worker can arrange transport but is asked to alter medication. What boundary applies?","Medication decisions require an appropriately qualified clinical role.",["Any coordination task grants prescribing authority.","Transport planning is itself a medical prescription.","The course badge replaces clinical authorisation."],"Practical assistance does not confer treatment competence."),
("An old directory does not verify current service fit.","A fictional referral option comes from a years-old leaflet. What should be checked through authorised arrangements?","Current scope, eligibility, access, cost and capacity relevant to the person's needs.",["Only the colour of its logo.","A universal acceptance guarantee from its name.","A real health history sent as a test."],"Service information can change and needs appropriate verification."),
("A scoping review is not a universal eligibility standard.","A learner applies one reviewed service's entry criteria to every mental-health service. What is missing?","Actual local service rules and context-specific verification.",["Permission to use any criterion because it was published.","Proof that all reviewed settings were identical.","A rule that no professional role differences exist."],"Examples from varied settings cannot establish universal criteria."),
("A proposed option differs from person-specific acceptance.","A service's general information is current but it has not accepted a fictional referral. What is the accurate status?","A potentially suitable verified option with person-specific acceptance still unresolved.",["Completed care and guaranteed recovery.","A confirmed booking solely from the directory.","Proof that no follow-up is needed."],"Information verification and an accepted referral are separate steps."),
]),
m("Permission handover and accepted responsibility","Specify a bounded handover with suitable information and confirmed responsibility.",[
"A referral should have a clear purpose that the person understands through appropriate communication. Permission to discuss options is not automatically permission to send a full record. Identify the minimum information needed for the specific receiving role, the relevant authority or consent arrangements and an approved channel. Do not promise confidentiality without limits or decide a real reporting exception from a classroom scenario. Clinical, safeguarding and local legal requirements may need qualified handling. A fictional handover note can name these questions without containing a real person's history or identifiers. [S1, S21]",
"Accepted responsibility matters at each stage. Clarify who sends the referral, who receives it, what confirmation is needed and who remains responsible while acceptance or access is unresolved. A message sent, a message received, a service accepted and assistance accessed are distinct statuses. The adult coordination review reports confusion, service gaps and workload barriers across settings, so a neat diagram cannot establish continuity. Agree a feasible next contact within authorised arrangements and explain limitations rather than assume silence means consent, attendance or a successful outcome. [R32, S21]",
"Routine referrals and urgent clinical or safeguarding concerns require different response arrangements. A worker should know the designated qualified route for the actual setting and use it when appropriate, rather than invent thresholds, assign a universal risk score or wait for a routine form to be processed. This course does not teach clinical triage, emergency treatment or physical intervention. The fictional exercise tests whether the learner recognises a scope boundary and responsible escalation dependency. It never asks learners to simulate a real crisis with an unprepared person or practise on someone in distress. [S21, S1]",
"Digital and peer options can be part of a support network, but they require appropriate scope, access, privacy, moderation and response arrangements. The 2026 digital peer-support review cannot validate an arbitrary group or guarantee that it will manage urgent needs. A person should be able to consider suitable alternatives without losing unrelated support. Record only necessary handover status in authorised systems, with realistic follow-up responsibility. If acceptance fails or preferences change, revisit options through the person and qualified roles; do not forward the same sensitive account more widely as an improvised solution. [R30, S21, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a worker sends a complete private account to several services and records a successful referral despite no acceptance. The adult had agreed only to discuss one option. The learner identifies the permission, minimum-information and responsibility gaps.",
"Draft a conditional handover outline with purpose, permission/authority check, necessary information, approved channel, sending/receiving responsibility, unresolved status and a qualified urgent-response boundary.",
"A defensible outline separates discussion from permission to share and sending from acceptance. It assigns responsibility for gaps, preserves information boundaries and recognises qualified urgent procedures.",["S1","S21","R32","R30"],[
("Discussing an option is not blanket consent to share records.","A fictional adult agrees to hear about a service, but a worker sends the full record. What should have been clarified?","The specific referral purpose, appropriate permission/authority and necessary information.",["Only whether the file was large enough.","An assumption that all helpful uses are authorised.","A public copy so other learners can check it."],"Information sharing needs its own appropriate basis and proportionate scope."),
("Sending and acceptance are different handover statuses.","A fictional referral email is sent but no service confirms acceptance. What remains necessary?","An assigned follow-up and clarity about responsibility while access is unresolved.",["Automatic completion of care.","A conclusion that the adult attended.","A rule that no sender has further duties after pressing send."],"Continuity needs accepted actions and follow-up rather than transmission alone."),
("Urgent concerns need the designated qualified response route.","A fictional support outline says all urgent concerns should wait for routine referral processing. What needs correction?","Use the setting's authorised qualified urgent clinical/safeguarding arrangements when applicable.",["Invent one numerical threshold for every situation.","Let an introductory quiz score authorise clinical triage.","Practise an unprepared real crisis scenario."],"Routine administration must not substitute for appropriate urgent procedures."),
("A peer group is not automatically a crisis service.","An unverified online peer forum is listed as the sole response to urgent clinical needs. What is missing?","Verified appropriate scope and qualified response arrangements with suitable alternatives.",["A popularity score sufficient to guarantee safety.","An assumption that all peers are clinicians.","A universal promise based on the review's title."],"Digital peer support has different roles and evidence limits from urgent clinical care."),
("Failed acceptance does not authorise wider disclosure.","One fictional service cannot accept a referral. What should a worker avoid?","Forwarding the full account to many others without appropriate purpose and permission.",["Revisiting suitable options with the person.","Recording the unresolved status appropriately.","Seeking qualified advice within authorised arrangements."],"A service gap does not remove information-sharing boundaries."),
]),
m("Transitions measurement and service improvement","Review access and continuity with defined measures and accountable action.",[
"Transitions between services can expose gaps in responsibility, communication or access. A person may change location, age eligibility or support preferences while a referral is pending. A useful review asks what the next service has accepted, what the person understands and who will respond if the planned contact fails. The WHO policy introduction connects these questions with staffing, governance and resources. Do not turn a transition failure into an assumption of non-compliance, or record support as completed because a form moved successfully between two systems. [S22, S21, S1]",
"Measure the question you actually need to answer. In an invented exercise, nine of twelve eligible handovers achieve a defined first contact in one period, compared with twelve of twenty in another. These are seventy-five percent and sixty percent: the count increases while the proportion falls by fifteen percentage points. Comparability still depends on the definition, eligible group, period and missing information. These fictional aggregates do not measure population mental-health prevalence, clinical recovery or staff competence. Unknown contact status should remain unknown under an explicit rule rather than automatically be recorded as success. [R32, S21]",
"The coordination scoping review identifies reported barriers and varied models but calls for rigorous effectiveness research. The digital peer-support review also has limited long-term and safety reporting. Neither permits a programme to infer recovery, universal safety or causal benefit from a contact count. An appropriate service review needs a relevant outcome, defined source, coverage, ethical governance and a design suited to its claim. Do not copy a clinical questionnaire or collect actual health records for this worksheet; critique the invented data and proposed process instead. [R32, R30, S1]",
"A review should support a feasible response. Identify the barrier, the role authorised to act, a resource or service dependency, an agreed feedback route and a point to revisit the change. Preserve uncertainty and differing perspectives, including those who could not use the proposed pathway. Where the solution needs a clinical, safeguarding, legal or organisational decision, state that dependency clearly. The course develops introductory coordination reasoning, not authority to approve a service or independently manage treatment. All changes to actual practice require appropriate qualified review, and observed skills remain a separate requirement for professional responsibilities. [S22, S21, S1]"],
"Fictional dataset: nine of twelve eligible handovers achieve first contact in period A; twelve of twenty do so in B. A report calls the larger second count an improved rate and proof of recovery. The learner corrects the arithmetic and separates contact from clinical outcomes.",
"Calculate both proportions and the percentage-point change. Add definition/coverage checks, an unknown-status rule, an unsupported claim to remove and an accountable improvement owner.",
"A is 75% and B is 60%, a decrease of 15 percentage points despite the higher count. First contact is a defined access observation, not demonstrated recovery or a causal programme effect.",["S22","S21","S1","R32","R30"],[
("Transition responsibility must remain explicit.","A fictional adult changes service eligibility while a referral is pending. What should be reviewed?","Accepted roles, suitable options and responsibility for the next contact or unresolved gap.",["A rule that all responsibility ends at an age boundary.","An assumption that another service automatically accepted.","A diagnosis of non-compliance from the administrative change."],"Continuity depends on actual arrangements during changing circumstances."),
("Counts and handover proportions can differ.","An invented service records nine of twelve first contacts, then twelve of twenty. What are the proportions?","Seventy-five percent followed by sixty percent.",["Sixty percent followed by seventy-five percent.","Nine percent followed by twelve percent.","Both are twelve percent because contacts are counted."],"9/12 is 0.75 and 12/20 is 0.60."),
("The percentage-point difference uses the two proportions.","A fictional first-contact proportion changes from 75% to 60%. What is the absolute change?","A decrease of fifteen percentage points.",["An increase of fifteen percentage points.","No change because the contact count increased.","A decrease of fifteen percent relative to 75%."],"The absolute difference is 60 minus 75 percentage points; relative change is different."),
("First contact is not demonstrated clinical recovery.","A fictional report calls an achieved first contact proof that mental-health difficulties resolved. What is justified?","A limited access observation, with clinical outcomes requiring separate appropriate evidence.",["A clinical cure from the appointment record alone.","A causal effect established by counting contacts.","A validated prevalence estimate for the whole community."],"The measure supports its defined contact status rather than broader health claims."),
("A review finding needs a feasible accountable response.","A fictional continuity review identifies a recurring unaccepted handover. What should a recommendation specify?","An authorised owner, corrective action, dependency, feedback route and review point.",["A success claim because more forms were sent.","Unrestricted linkage of all health records.","A promise that one worker can resolve every system gap."],"Service improvement connects a supported finding with authority, resources and follow-through."),
])])
